urface, in meters? Round your answer to one decimal place.Rounded to one decimal place, the radius is approximately\n{{field-1}} meters."}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Length From a Physical Description" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050203 :topic/math-academy-id 6548 :topic/title "Descriptions of Physical Phenomena"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225154 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224758}] :question/math-academy-id "q-323905" :question/problem "The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the product of the spring constant, in newtons per meter, and the extension of the spring, in meters. A spring has a natural length of $0.4$ meters, and when a load is applied, the spring is stretched to a length of $0.6$ meters. If the spring constant is $150$ newtons per meter, what is the force exerted on the spring, in newtons?The force is\n{{field-1}} newtons."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224759}] :question/math-academy-id "q-323907" :question/problem "The force exerted on a stretched spring is defined as the product of the spring constant, in Newtons per meter, and the extension of the spring, in meters. A spring has a natural length of $0.4$ meters. If the spring constant is $200$ newtons per meter and the spring experiences a force of $50$ newtons, what is the stretched length of the spring, in meters?The stretched length of the spring is\n{{field-1}} meters."}] :knowledge-point/title "Rates of Change in Physical Phenomena" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050203 :topic/math-academy-id 6548 :topic/title "Descriptions of Physical Phenomena"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225155 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/r